<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahaziah of Judah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 841 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahaziah was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> for about a year around 842–841 BCE, the son of Jehoram and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athaliah">Athaliah</a>. While visiting his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoram-of-israel">Joram of Israel</a> at Jezreel, he was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a>'s coup, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan">Tel Dan</a> Stele, probably set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazael">Hazael</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, claims these deaths for the Aramean king. His mother Athaliah then seized the throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahaziah-of-judah.jpg" alt="Ahaziah of Judah" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Guillaume Rouille (1518?-1589) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahaziah_of_Judah.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ahaziah, also called Jehoahaz in 2 Chronicles, was the sixth king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>. He was the son of Jehoram and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athaliah">Athaliah</a>, the daughter or possibly the sister of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab of Israel</a>. This made him the first Judahite king descended from both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omride-dynasty">House of Omri</a>. He was the youngest son of Jehoram, since according to 2 Chronicles his older brothers had been carried off in a Philistine and Arab raid. Biblical sources disagree on his age at accession: 2 Kings gives 22 and 2 Chronicles 42.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Under the influence of his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athaliah">Athaliah</a>, Ahaziah introduced forms of worship that offended the Yahwistic party. Members of her family became his advisers and encouraged him to join his uncle Jehoram, king of Israel, in an expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazael">Hazael</a>, king of the Arameans. Jehoram was wounded in the battle and went to recover at Jezreel. Ahaziah left the field in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilead">Gilead</a>, visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and then came to Jezreel for a conference with Jehoram, where he was caught up in the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a>.</p><p>According to 2 Kings, Ahaziah and Jehoram both went out to meet the rebellious general. Ahaziah watched Jehoram shot by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a> with a bow, and fled, but was wounded at the pass of Gur in Ibleam and reached only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a>, where he died. 2 Chronicles gives a different account, in which Ahaziah hid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> after Jehu's coup and was found and killed by Jehu's men on their king's orders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Tel Dan Stele, whose fragments were found in 1993 and 1994, claims that its author killed both the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a>, Ahaziah, and the king of Israel, Jehoram. Its most likely author is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazael">Hazael</a> of the Arameans. The inscription may be a contemporary witness, but kings of that time often boasted and exaggerated, and it is not clear whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a> killed the two kings, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> reports, or Hazael did, or whether Jehu acted in concert with Hazael.</p><p>The calendars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> and Israel were offset by six months, with the Judean year starting in Tishri and that of Israel in Nisan. For Ahaziah the biblical data narrow his accession to between Nisan 1 and the day before Tishri 1 of 841 BCE. William F. Albright dated his reign to 842 BCE, and E. R. Thiele to 841/840, a date later scholars moved one year earlier. Leslie McFall proposed a one-year coregency with his father Jehoram, who had fallen ill, to explain the differing synchronisms in 2 Kings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahaziah of Judah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jehu, an army commander, overthrew it c. 841 BCE in a coup supported by the prophet Elisha, killing King Joram of Israel and King <b>Ahaziah of Judah</b>, although the Tel Dan Stele, probably set up by Hazael of Damascus, claims those deaths for the Aramean king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in Tel Megiddo, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=-851" aria-label="Jerusalem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5458 1007 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ahaziah of Judah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5908 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5908 1277h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=-851">What was here in 851 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
